-- title:
--   Definitions for Energy-constrained diamond norm with applications to the uniform continuity of continuous variable channel capacities
-- statement:
--   The objects the formalized statements of *Energy-constrained diamond norm with applications to the uniform continuity of continuous variable channel capacities* (arXiv:1712.10267) are stated in. Each was checked against the paper by a blind read-back and a moderator.
--
--   - `IsSuperoperator`: Sections I and III, PDF pp.1--4: real span of bounded CP maps on trace class. Every bounded CP map is a nonnegative multiple of a trace-nonincreasing quantum operation. Equality is imposed only on the actual trace-class domain.
--   - `Grounded`: Section III, PDF p.4: infimum energy zero, without assuming a ground vector. The dense form domain, nonnegativity and closedness belong to H.
--   - `HasGroundVector`: Section V, PDF p.9: actual zero-energy ground vector, used at budget zero.
--   - `diamondMagnitude`: Equation (1), PDF p.1: full stabilized trace norm. A countably infinite reference contains every separable reference isometrically, including finite ones. No copy-reference or pure-state reduction is built into this definition.
--   - `constrainedMagnitude`: Equation (2), PDF p.4: full stabilized norm with an input-marginal energy functional. Only the first input is constrained; neither reference nor output is. The empty supremum is zero; positive budgets and grounding exclude emptiness.
--   - `energyMagnitude`: Definition 3, PDF p.4, for a general closed positive form.
--   - `pureEnergyMagnitude`: Section III, PDF p.4: pure probes on a copy of the input.
--   - `tensorSumEnergy`: Lemma 4(5), PDF p.5: expectation of the additive tensor Hamiltonian on positive joint inputs, defined through actual partial traces.
--   - `CanonicalSuperoperator`: Lemma 4(2), PDF p.5: canonical representatives of maps whose domain is trace class. Off-domain zero is representation bookkeeping, not a restriction.
--   - `energyNormTopology`: Lemma 4(2), PDF p.5: topology generated by all translated norm distances.
--   - `vacuumAttenuator`: Sections II and IV.B, PDF pp.2,8: complex-amplitude vacuum attenuator in the full number basis. This Kraus sum includes eta=0 and the unit-circle boundary; powers at exponent zero are one, including 0^0. No photon cutoff is made.
--   - `numberEnergy`: Section IV.B, PDF p.8: extended number expectation on positive inputs.
--   - `diagonalEvolution`: Section II, PDF pp.3--4: actual conjugation by the diagonal spectral unitary exp(-itH), on the entire trace class.
--   - `diagonalUnitaryDistance`: Section II, PDF p.4: operator norm distance from identity of a diagonal unitary. The supremum of the diagonal moduli is its full Hilbert operator norm.
--   - `maximumEntropy`: Section V, PDF pp.4,8: entropy of the maximum-entropy state under a budget. On the Gibbs domain this supremum is finite and equals S(gamma_H(E)), including zero budget and finite systems; it avoids a fictitious finite inverse temperature at the ground-state endpoint or above the maximally mixed finite-system energy.
--   - `AffineEnergyLimited`: Section V, PDF p.9: affine form inequality, on every finite-energy state. It is not a Banach adjoint evaluated at an unbounded observable.
--   - `outputEnergyEnvelope`: Section V, PDF p.9: output-energy envelope, with infinity retained.
--   - `conditionalEntropyValue`: Section V, PDF pp.8--11: conditional entropy S(first)-I(first:second), converted only on its finite-first-entropy domain. Gibbs output budgets in the statements ensure finiteness; no entropy assumption on the reference is imposed.
--   - `blockHolevo`: Section V, PDF p.11: block Holevo supremum over finite mixed ensembles. The average INPUT energy is bounded; refinement gives the pure-ensemble value.
--   - `energyConstrainedBlockCoherentInformation`: Section V, PDF p.11: coherent information optimized at the constrained input. With finite input entropy use I(input:output)-S(input). If input entropy is infinite and output entropy is finite, coherent information is minus infinity; its nonnegative part is zero. The explicit branch prevents the frozen finite-input adapter from converting infinite input entropy to zero before subtraction. Pure feasible inputs give zero, so clipping preserves the supremum.
--   - `energyConstrainedClassicalCapacity`: Section V, PDF p.11: regularized constrained classical capacity, in nats. Positive block lengths only. Empty suprema are zero; Gibbs bounds make these capacities finite in the theorems before the real conversion is used.
--   - `energyConstrainedQuantumCapacity`: Section V, PDF p.11: regularized constrained quantum capacity, in nats.
--   - `blockEntropyBound`: Lemma 8, equation (7), PDF p.10: the sharper first right side. Printed quotients at error zero are undefined; the zero branch is their continuous extension, reflecting identical channels. Independent correction review pending.
--   - `simpleEntropyBound`: Lemma 8, equation (7), PDF p.10: simplified right side, with the same explicit continuous extension at zero error.
--   - `capacityBound`: Theorem 9, PDF p.12: both capacity bounds are twice the simplified conditional-entropy modulus. This gives exactly constants 56 and 6.
-- source:
--   Energy-constrained diamond norm with applications to the uniform continuity of continuous variable channel capacities, arXiv:1712.10267v1, https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.10267v1

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Claimed50_P13
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Claimed50_P18
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Claimed50_P35
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Claimed50_P39

/-!
Winter, arXiv:1712.10267v1. Full trace-class operators in countable orthonormal
coordinates; product coordinates are completed Hilbert tensor products.
PositiveObservable is a dense closed nonnegative quadratic form and permits
continuous spectrum. All entropy quantities use natural logarithms.
No theorem below claims infinite-dimensional attainment. Deferred source and
interface nodes are listed individually in HARD.md and GAPS.json.
Compilation and independent moderation are pending the batch handoff.
-/

noncomputable section

open Filter
open scoped ENNReal NNReal BigOperators Topology

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Bridge50.P23

open QAlgorithms.RealRegisters (Hilbert)
open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Dependency50.P33
  (State PureState IsTraceClass traceSize tensorApply rankOne productCoeff)
open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Frontier18.P01
  (Channel traceSecond traceFirst homogeneousEntropy generalizedRelativeEntropy
   channelTensorAction finiteInputCoherentInfo)
open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Frontier18.P08 (IsQuantumOperation)
open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Claimed50.P13
  (PositiveObservable closedFormExpectation GibbsCondition TensorBlockIndex blockAction blockEnergy)
open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Claimed50.P18 (finiteFirstConditionalEntropy)
open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Claimed50.P39 (g)

attribute [local instance] Classical.propDecidable Classical.decEq

/-! Accepted-library comparison for the three capacity-name repair targets:
Support50.P01.blockCoherentInformation (line 48) is the unoptimized coherent
information of a finite-dimensional dilation, with no Hamiltonian or budget.
Information100.P62.classicalCapacity/quantumCapacity (lines 157/159) are finite
zero-error graph capacities in bits. Information100.P22.quantumCapacity (line 872)
and Information100.P80.quantumCapacity (line 98) also require finite registers,
are unconstrained, and use bits. Claimed50.P35.traceClassQuantumCapacity is
unconstrained and in qubits. None can replace the nats-valued energy-constrained
optimizations on p.11. Claimed50.P13.regularizedUnassistedCapacity is a related
classical liminf regularization; retaining the printed positive-block supremum
keeps this draft's formula unchanged. The three distinct constrained concepts
therefore have explicit energyConstrained names below. Their bodies reuse the
already imported frozen finiteInputCoherentInfo, blockAction, blockEnergy and
finiteEnsembleCapacity; no frozen definition is restated or edited. -/

/-- Sections I and III, PDF pp.1--4: real span of bounded CP maps on trace
class. Every bounded CP map is a nonnegative multiple of a trace-nonincreasing
quantum operation. Equality is imposed only on the actual trace-class domain. -/
def IsSuperoperator {I J : Type} (D : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ) : Prop :=
  ∃ (a b : ℝ) (P Q : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ),
    0 ≤ a ∧ 0 ≤ b ∧ IsQuantumOperation P ∧ IsQuantumOperation Q ∧
    ∀ X, IsTraceClass X → D X = (a : ℂ) • P X - (b : ℂ) • Q X

/-- Section III, PDF p.4: infimum energy zero, without assuming a ground
vector. The dense form domain, nonnegativity and closedness belong to H. -/
def Grounded {I : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) : Prop :=
  ∀ e : ℝ, 0 < e → ∃ u : Hilbert I, ‖u‖ = 1 ∧ H.form u ≤ ENNReal.ofReal e

/-- Section V, PDF p.9: actual zero-energy ground vector, used at budget zero. -/
def HasGroundVector {I : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) : Prop :=
  ∃ u : Hilbert I, ‖u‖ = 1 ∧ H.form u = 0

/-- Equation (1), PDF p.1: full stabilized trace norm. A countably infinite
reference contains every separable reference isometrically, including finite ones.
No copy-reference or pure-state reduction is built into this definition. -/
def diamondMagnitude {I J : Type} (D : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ rho : State (I × ℕ), traceSize (tensorApply D rho.coefficients)

/-- Equation (2), PDF p.4: full stabilized norm with an input-marginal energy
functional. Only the first input is constrained; neither reference nor output is.
The empty supremum is zero; positive budgets and grounding exclude emptiness. -/
def constrainedMagnitude {I J : Type} (F : Matrix I I ℂ → ℝ≥0∞) (E : ℝ)
    (D : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ rho : {rho : State (I × ℕ) //
    F (traceSecond rho.coefficients) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal E},
    traceSize (tensorApply D rho.val.coefficients)

/-- Definition 3, PDF p.4, for a general closed positive form. -/
def energyMagnitude {I J : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (E : ℝ)
    (D : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  constrainedMagnitude (closedFormExpectation H) E D

/-- Section III, PDF p.4: pure probes on a copy of the input. -/
def pureEnergyMagnitude {I J : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (E : ℝ)
    (D : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ rho : {rho : PureState (I × I) //
    closedFormExpectation H (traceSecond rho.val.coefficients) ≤ ENNReal.ofReal E},
    traceSize (tensorApply D rho.val.val.coefficients)

/-- Lemma 4(5), PDF p.5: expectation of the additive tensor Hamiltonian on
positive joint inputs, defined through actual partial traces. -/
def tensorSumEnergy {I K : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (G : PositiveObservable K)
    (X : Matrix (I × K) (I × K) ℂ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  closedFormExpectation H (traceSecond X) + closedFormExpectation G (traceFirst X)

/-- Lemma 4(2), PDF p.5: canonical representatives of maps whose domain is
trace class. Off-domain zero is representation bookkeeping, not a restriction. -/
abbrev CanonicalSuperoperator (I J : Type) :=
  {D : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ // IsSuperoperator D ∧
    ∀ X, ¬ IsTraceClass X → D X = 0}

/-- Lemma 4(2), PDF p.5: topology generated by all translated norm distances. -/
def energyNormTopology {I J : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (E : ℝ) :
    TopologicalSpace (CanonicalSuperoperator I J) :=
  TopologicalSpace.induced
    (fun D : CanonicalSuperoperator I J => fun T : CanonicalSuperoperator I J =>
      energyMagnitude H E (fun X => D.val X - T.val X)) inferInstance

/-- Sections II and IV.B, PDF pp.2,8: complex-amplitude vacuum attenuator in
the full number basis. This Kraus sum includes eta=0 and the unit-circle boundary;
powers at exponent zero are one, including 0^0. No photon cutoff is made. -/
def vacuumAttenuator (eta : ℂ) (X : Matrix ℕ ℕ ℂ) : Matrix ℕ ℕ ℂ :=
  fun m n => ∑' l : ℕ,
    ((Real.sqrt (Nat.choose (m + l) l : ℝ) *
      Real.sqrt (Nat.choose (n + l) l : ℝ) * (1 - ‖eta‖ ^ 2) ^ l : ℝ) : ℂ) *
      eta ^ m * star eta ^ n * X (m + l) (n + l)

/-- Section IV.B, PDF p.8: extended number expectation on positive inputs. -/
def numberEnergy (X : Matrix ℕ ℕ ℂ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ∑' n : ℕ, (n : ℝ≥0∞) * ENNReal.ofReal (X n n).re

/-- Section II, PDF pp.3--4: actual conjugation by the diagonal spectral
unitary exp(-itH), on the entire trace class. -/
def diagonalEvolution {I : Type} (energy : I → ℝ) (t : ℝ)
    (X : Matrix I I ℂ) : Matrix I I ℂ :=
  fun i j => Complex.exp (-Complex.I * (t * (energy i - energy j) : ℝ)) * X i j

/-- Section II, PDF p.4: operator norm distance from identity of a diagonal
unitary. The supremum of the diagonal moduli is its full Hilbert operator norm. -/
def diagonalUnitaryDistance (energy : ℕ → ℝ) (t : ℝ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ n : ℕ, ENNReal.ofReal ‖Complex.exp (-Complex.I * (t * energy n : ℝ)) - 1‖

/-- Section V, PDF pp.4,8: entropy of the maximum-entropy state under a budget.
On the Gibbs domain this supremum is finite and equals S(gamma_H(E)), including
zero budget and finite systems; it avoids a fictitious finite inverse temperature
at the ground-state endpoint or above the maximally mixed finite-system energy. -/
def maximumEntropy {I : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (E : ℝ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ rho : {rho : State I // closedFormExpectation H rho.coefficients ≤ ENNReal.ofReal E},
    homogeneousEntropy rho.val.coefficients

/-- Section V, PDF p.9: affine form inequality, on every finite-energy state.
It is not a Banach adjoint evaluated at an unbounded observable. -/
def AffineEnergyLimited {I J : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (G : PositiveObservable J)
    (alpha offset : ℝ) (N : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ) : Prop :=
  ∀ rho : State I, closedFormExpectation H rho.coefficients < ⊤ →
    closedFormExpectation G (N rho.coefficients) ≤
      ENNReal.ofReal alpha * closedFormExpectation H rho.coefficients + ENNReal.ofReal offset

/-- Section V, PDF p.9: output-energy envelope, with infinity retained. -/
def outputEnergyEnvelope {I J : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (G : PositiveObservable J)
    (N : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ) (E : ℝ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ rho : {rho : State I // closedFormExpectation H rho.coefficients ≤ ENNReal.ofReal E},
    closedFormExpectation G (N rho.val.coefficients)

/-- Section V, PDF pp.8--11: conditional entropy S(first)-I(first:second),
converted only on its finite-first-entropy domain. Gibbs output budgets in the
statements ensure finiteness; no entropy assumption on the reference is imposed. -/
def conditionalEntropyValue {I C : Type} (X : Matrix (I × C) (I × C) ℂ) : ℝ :=
  (finiteFirstConditionalEntropy X).toReal

/-- Section V, PDF p.11: block Holevo supremum over finite mixed ensembles.
The average INPUT energy is bounded; refinement gives the pure-ensemble value. -/
def blockHolevo {I J : Type} (N : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ)
    (H : PositiveObservable I) (n : ℕ) (E : ℝ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Frontier18.P07.finiteEnsembleCapacity (blockAction N n)
    {rho | blockEnergy H n rho.coefficients ≤ ENNReal.ofReal E}

/-- Section V, PDF p.11: coherent information optimized at the constrained
input. With finite input entropy use I(input:output)-S(input). If input
entropy is infinite and output entropy is finite, coherent information is minus
infinity; its nonnegative part is zero. The explicit branch prevents the frozen
finite-input adapter from converting infinite input entropy to zero before
subtraction. Pure feasible inputs give zero, so clipping preserves the supremum. -/
def energyConstrainedBlockCoherentInformation {I J : Type} (N : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ)
    (H : PositiveObservable I) (n : ℕ) (E : ℝ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ rho : {rho : State (TensorBlockIndex I n) //
    blockEnergy H n rho.coefficients ≤ ENNReal.ofReal E},
    if homogeneousEntropy rho.val.coefficients = ⊤ then 0 else
      ENNReal.ofReal (finiteInputCoherentInfo rho.val.coefficients (blockAction N n))

/-- Section V, PDF p.11: regularized constrained classical capacity, in nats.
Positive block lengths only. Empty suprema are zero; Gibbs bounds make these
capacities finite in the theorems before the real conversion is used. -/
def energyConstrainedClassicalCapacity {I J : Type} (N : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ)
    (H : PositiveObservable I) (E : ℝ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ n : {n : ℕ // 0 < n}, (n.val : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * blockHolevo N H n.val ((n.val : ℝ) * E)

/-- Section V, PDF p.11: regularized constrained quantum capacity, in nats. -/
def energyConstrainedQuantumCapacity {I J : Type} (N : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ)
    (H : PositiveObservable I) (E : ℝ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨆ n : {n : ℕ // 0 < n}, (n.val : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ *
    energyConstrainedBlockCoherentInformation N H n.val ((n.val : ℝ) * E)

/-- Lemma 8, equation (7), PDF p.10: the sharper first right side. Printed
quotients at error zero are undefined; the zero branch is their continuous
extension, reflecting identical channels. Independent correction review pending. -/
def blockEntropyBound {J : Type} (G : PositiveObservable J) (F eps : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if eps = 0 then 0 else
    let d := Real.sqrt eps
    14 * d * (1 + d) * (maximumEntropy G ((1 + 3 * d) * F / d)).toReal +
      3 * g (2 * d * (1 + d))

/-- Lemma 8, equation (7), PDF p.10: simplified right side, with the same
explicit continuous extension at zero error. -/
def simpleEntropyBound {J : Type} (G : PositiveObservable J) (F eps : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if eps = 0 then 0 else
    let d := Real.sqrt eps
    28 * d * (maximumEntropy G (4 * F / d)).toReal + 3 * g (4 * d)

/-- Theorem 9, PDF p.12: both capacity bounds are twice the simplified
conditional-entropy modulus. This gives exactly constants 56 and 6. -/
def capacityBound {J : Type} (G : PositiveObservable J) (F eps : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  2 * simpleEntropyBound G F eps

/-! Sanity examples pin the local body definitions and endpoint conventions. -/

example {I J : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (E : ℝ)
    (D : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ) :
    energyMagnitude H E D = constrainedMagnitude (closedFormExpectation H) E D := rfl

example {I K : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (G : PositiveObservable K)
    (X : Matrix (I × K) (I × K) ℂ) :
    tensorSumEnergy H G X = closedFormExpectation H (traceSecond X) +
      closedFormExpectation G (traceFirst X) := rfl

example {I : Type} (energy : I → ℝ) (X : Matrix I I ℂ) :
    diagonalEvolution energy 0 X = X := by
  funext i j
  simp [diagonalEvolution]

example (X : Matrix ℕ ℕ ℂ) : vacuumAttenuator 0 X 0 0 = ∑' n, X n n := by
  simp [vacuumAttenuator]

example {J : Type} (G : PositiveObservable J) (F : ℝ) :
    blockEntropyBound G F 0 = 0 ∧ simpleEntropyBound G F 0 = 0 ∧ capacityBound G F 0 = 0 := by
  simp [blockEntropyBound, simpleEntropyBound, capacityBound]

example {I J : Type} (P : Matrix I I ℂ → Matrix J J ℂ)
    (hP : IsQuantumOperation P) : IsSuperoperator (fun _ : Matrix I I ℂ => (0 : Matrix J J ℂ)) := by
  refine ⟨0, 0, P, P, le_rfl, le_rfl, hP, hP, ?_⟩
  intro X hX
  simp

example {I : Type} (H : PositiveObservable I) (h : HasGroundVector H) : Grounded H := by
  obtain ⟨u, hu, hzero⟩ := h
  intro e he
  exact ⟨u, hu, by simp [hzero]⟩

example {I : Type} (energy : I → ℝ) (t : ℝ) (X : Matrix I I ℂ) (i : I) :
    diagonalEvolution energy t X i i = X i i := by
  simp [diagonalEvolution]

end QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Bridge50.P23


